<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Barghash bin Said</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1837–<a href="/years/1888">1888</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Barghash bin Said of Zanzibar</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Barghash bin Said was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> of Zanzibar from 1870 to 1888, a son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/said-bin-sultan">Said bin Sultan</a>. Under British pressure he signed the treaty of 1873 that closed the Zanzibar slave market. He built palaces including the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-wonders">House of Wonders</a>, introduced piped water and steamships, and claimed a long stretch of the East African coast, but lost much of it to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scramble-for-africa">scramble for Africa</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/barghash-bin-said.jpg" alt="Barghash bin Said" width="320" height="463" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Maull &amp; Co. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Barghash_bin_Said_of_Zanzibar.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
